\section{Early Training Signal Analysis}
\label{app:early_signal}

We investigated whether early training signals (steps 1--3) can predict seed-level generalization outcomes. Across 12 seeds spanning three outcome categories---Generalized (OOD $>$ 20\%), Stagnant (OOD $\approx$ SFT baseline), and Collapsed (OOD $<$ SFT)---no standard training metric (reward, KL divergence, entropy, gradient norm, response length, or train success rate) showed statistically significant between-group differences in the first three training steps (all Welch $t$-test $p > 0.2$, $n = 4$ per group). Two of the four Collapsed seeds (seed7, seed8) exhibited among the \textit{highest} early rewards and train success rates, with their collapse manifesting only after step~5 through response length inflation or reward degradation.

The strongest candidate predictor was the mutual information diversity estimate at step~1, which showed a large effect size (Cohen's $d = 1.11$) favoring Generalized over Collapsed seeds and a trending correlation with final OOD performance (Spearman $\rho = 0.43$, $p = 0.19$), but remained non-significant with the available sample size ($n = 4$ per group). These results suggest that seed-dependent outcomes are governed by mechanisms not captured by standard early training diagnostics, and that early stopping or seed selection based on initial step metrics would be unreliable.

